### B-1 (B633-1): the scanner sees every fenced block
- Design (the drafter's; deviate only with a measurement in DESIGN): `shellCommands` keeps its signature and returns the same `{ line, text }` shape; it recognises a fence line after ANY leading whitespace (spaces or tabs), opening with 3 or more backticks (tilde fences too if you choose, with a planted row; DEPLOY.md holds none: say which); the info string is trimmed and its first word decides (empty, `sh`, `bash`, `shell`, `zsh` = scan; anything else = skip); **the open and close of every block, shell or not, is tracked** (a closing fence is a fence line of the same character, at least as long, holding nothing else after trimming; it need not match the opening line's indentation), so a non-shell block can never invert the toggle; a block still open at the section end is dropped, not scanned past the heading. Comment lines (first non-blank character `#`) and `\` continuations behave as now. Do not scan prose. The doc comment above `shellCommands` and the title/comment of the DEPLOY row must be TRUE of the code afterwards.
- Remove the ids `B616 (B620-1)` from the header comment (B633-8); keep the explanation of why the file exists, in words.
- **Rows (additions only; the two existing rows keep their titles and every existing assertion; no `expect(` removed or loosened; new titles carry no brief, gate, finding or ticket id):** (i) a row that feeds P1, P2, P3, P4 (and P5) as planted strings and expects each flagged with the right line; (ii) **durable planted-text rows over the real file's text, in memory:** read DEPLOY.md, insert by program an indented sh block holding a double-quoted bang right after the 0026 heading's section start and again inside the 0027 section (and once as a column-0 block in each), and expect the scan flagged in each section and the real unplanted text unflagged (control: the same function on the real text returns none); (iii) a row for each shape that must NOT be flagged: a bang in single quotes in an indented block, a bang in prose, a bang in a `text` block that follows a sh block, a bang in a comment line, a bang in a block after `## A later migration` and in one before the first heading (this pins the section bounds; if you do not add the last two, say so and argue S10 equivalent). Expected added rows: state the number and the titles in STATUS BEFORE the first run (the drafter suggests 4 to 8).

### B-2 (B633-2): the control cases and the guard
- Control cases (strings, no file): a bang NOT followed by `~` inside double quotes (exactly `-c "x!y"` and `-c "SELECT 'x!y'"`), flagged (kills S04); a bang after a single quote inside double quotes, flagged (kills S05: the `'` is plain text inside double quotes); a bang in a double-quoted span that follows a closed single-quoted span (`'a' "b!c"`), flagged; an escaped quote inside a double-quoted span keeps the span open (`"d\"e!"`), flagged; none of these flagged when the bang sits in a single-quoted span only.
- **The guard (S11):** the "the scan must see the blocks" expectation must be exercised by a control row so that deleting it, wherever it sits, turns a row red: put the guard INSIDE the function both the DEPLOY row and a control call (for example `scanDeploy(markdown)` that THROWS, with a message naming the headings, when the two sections yield no command holding `psql`, and otherwise returns the findings), keep the DEPLOY row's call and its title as they are, and add a control row feeding a section whose blocks the scan cannot see (a prose-only section; a section whose only block is a `text` block) and expecting the throw. Say in DESIGN which forms S11 takes in the new code (guard dropped inside the function; call-site expectation dropped) and show each killed.
- No weakening: the first existing row must still be red on a planted bang in the real text and green on the real head file.

